Yukti

You are correct that multispecies surveys, particularly with additional stratification can present problems when subsetting data.  This can be caused by the subsetting resulting in aspects of the study design not being respected.  For example, some transects in one of your habitats may not have had detections; if care is not taken, the Distance package will not realise those transects were surveyed, resulting in incorrect calculation of effort and an overestimate of density.  Fortunately for you, some error messages were thrown to cause you to look more closely.

I have written another vignette dealing with the multi-species/multi-strata issue (but not involving covariates) that shows how to provide details of your survey design to the Distance package such that these effort miscalculations are avoided.  The flatfile data format is supplemented with explicit information in the form of region.table​ and sample.table​ arguments to the ds​ function.

The vignette uses the same Montrave songbird data set as the species-covariate​ vignette.  This vignette is not yet available on our examples​ page as it is undergoing review by the distance sampling team.  Have a look at this temporary location:
http://distancelive.xyz/multispecies-multioccasion-analysis.html
Perils of multispecies and multisession distance sampling analysis <http://distancelive.xyz/multispecies-multioccasion-analysis.html>
Example using Montrave data employing `region.table` and `sample.table` construct
distancelive.xyz <http://distancelive.xyz>

