Richard

I'm not an expert in DSM nor GIS so this isn't likely to be a high quality answer.

Nevertheless, I recognise the plotting code as coming from a DSM vignette produced by some of our French colleagues

http://distancesampling.org/R/vignettes/hare_point_transect_dsm.html
MailScanner has detected a possible fraud attempt from "distancesampling.org" claiming to be DSM using point transect data - distancesampling.org <http://distancesampling.org/R/vignettes/hare_point_transect_dsm.html>
## Warning: package 'knitr' was built under R version 3.3.3. In France, the French National Agency for Hunting and Wildlife has recently created the Hare network (Mauvy et al. 2017), a network of voluntary sites where hares and foxes are sampled every year in February using point transect sampling (Péroux et al. 1997).. As an illustration, we used the sampling data from Rouillacais for hare ...
distancesampling.org <http://distancesampling.org>
The essential objects that you want is either the object created by the function  grid_plot_obj()​ or the constituent pieces passed as arguments to that function ( `pred.grid` and mod_tw_pred​).

Afraid I don't know how to export them into GIS layers; the GIS gurus may have thoughts about this.
